\label{l:Lemma_for_Prop1}
For binary-valued random variables \(R,S,T\) and any two bits \(\gamma,\delta\), we have: 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}(R\oplus T = \gamma\oplus\delta\oplus 1)\leqslant\mathbb{P}(R\oplus S=\gamma) + \mathbb{P}(S\oplus T=\delta).
\end{equation}

Consider a strategy where two players respond deterministically on input \(X\). Define $W_i$ to be the event corresponding to satisfaction of the predicate defined in~\eqref{e:5game} for which the $i$-th player is the (sole) player measuring $Z$; we use $W_f$ to distinguish the final all-$Z$ predicate from the cyclically equivalent first five. We prove \(\mathcal{S}:=\mathbb{P}(W_f)+\sum_{i=1}^{5}\mathbb{P}(W_i)\leqslant 5\), which implies the average predicate-winning probability $\omega$ satisfies \(\omega\leqslant 5/6\). Let \(e_i\coloneqq\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm{c}}_i)\), for \(1\leqslant i\leqslant 5\), and \(e_f\coloneqq\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm{c}}_f)\). Let the sum of the failure probabilities of the cyclic three-player predicates be \(e_T=\sum_{i=1}^{5}e_i\). The total probability of failure is then \(e=e_f+e_T\). We first prove the result for a pair of adjacent players \((\mathsf{A}_1,\mathsf{A}_2)\), following which we prove it for a non-adjacent pair \((\mathsf{A}_1,\mathsf{A}_3)\).

Suppose \(A^{(1)}_X=\alpha\) and \(A^{(2)}_X=\beta\) deterministically. Then \(\mathbb{P}(W_2)=\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_Z\oplus A^{(3)}_X=1\oplus\alpha)=1-e_2\), and \(\mathbb{P}(W_4)=\mathbb{P}(A^{(3)}_X\oplus A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_X=1)=1-e_4\). Using Lemma~\ref{l:Lemma_for_Prop1}, we have 
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_X=1\oplus\alpha)\leqslant\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_Z\oplus A^{(3)}_X=\alpha)+\mathbb{P}(A^{(3)}_X\oplus A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_X=0)=e_2+e_4,
\end{equation*}
implying that \(\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_X=\alpha)\geqslant 1-(e_2 + e_4)\). Again, because of the fixed outcomes, we have \(\mathbb{P}(W_3)=\mathbb{P}(A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_X=1\oplus\beta)=1-e_3\) and \(\mathbb{P}(W_5)=\mathbb{P}(A^{(4)}_X\oplus A^{(5)}_Z=1\oplus\alpha)=1-e_5\), and following a similar usage of the same lemma, we get \(\mathbb{P}(A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_Z=\alpha\oplus\beta)\geqslant 1-(e_3 + e_5)\). For the remainder of the proof, ``combining'' two parity probabilities involves the usage of Lemma~\ref{l:Lemma_for_Prop1} and the union bound on probabilities. Combining \(\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm c}_1)=e_1\) and \(\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_X=1\oplus\alpha)\leqslant e_2+e_4\), we get \(\mathbb{P}(A^{(1)}_Z\oplus A^{(2)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_z=\alpha\oplus\beta)\geqslant1-(e_1+e_2+e_4)\). Combining the probability of the complement of event \(\{A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_Z=\alpha\oplus\beta\}\) (which holds with probability at least \(1-(e_3+e_5)\)) and \(\mathbb{P}(A^{(1)}_Z\oplus A^{(2)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_Z=\alpha\oplus\beta)\geqslant1-(e_1+e_2+e_4)\), we get
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{P}\Big(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=0\Big)\leqslant \sum_{i=1}^{5}e_i \Rightarrow \mathbb{P}\Big(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=1\Big)\geqslant 1-\sum_{i=1}^{5}e_i=1-e_T,
\end{equation*}
comparing which with \(\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm c}_f)=\mathbb{P}(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=0)=e_f\), we find that the target parities are opposite. Therefore, combining them gives us:
\begin{equation*}
1 = \mathbb{P}\Big(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=0\Big) + \mathbb{P}\Big(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=1\Big) \geqslant 1 - e_f + 1-e_T=2-e,
\end{equation*}
implying that \(e\geqslant 1\). This shows that \(\mathcal{S}=6-e\leqslant 5\), equivalently, \(\omega\leqslant 5/6\).

Now assume that \(A^{(1)}_X=\gamma\) and \(A^{(3)}_X=\delta\). Since, \(\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm c}_2)=e_2\), we have \(\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_Z=\gamma\oplus\delta)=e_2\). Combining \(\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm c}_4)=\mathbb{P}(A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_X=\delta)=e_4\) and \(\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm c}_1)=\mathbb{P}(A^{(1)}_Z\oplus A^{(2)}_X\oplus A^{(5)}_X=0)=e_1\): 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:2FixedPlayers_1}
\mathbb{P}(A^{(1)}_Z\oplus A^{(2)}_X\oplus A^{(4)}_Z=\delta)\leqslant e_1 + e_4.
\end{equation}
Combining \(\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm{c}}_3)=\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_X\oplus A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_X=0)=e_3\) and \(\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm{c}}_5)=\mathbb{P}(A^{(4)}_X\oplus A^{(5)}_Z=\gamma)=e_5\):
\begin{equation}\label{eq:2FixedPlayers_2}
\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_X\oplus A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_Z=\gamma)\leqslant e_3 + e_5.
\end{equation}
Combining~\eqref{eq:2FixedPlayers_1} and~\eqref{eq:2FixedPlayers_2}, we have \(\mathbb{P}(A^{(1)}_Z\oplus A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_Z=\gamma\oplus\delta)\leqslant e_1+e_3+e_4+e_5\), which in combination with \(\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_Z=\gamma\oplus\delta)=e_2\) gives:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}\Big(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=0\Big)\leqslant\sum_{i=1}^{5}e_i \Rightarrow \mathbb{P}\Big(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=1\Big)\geqslant 1-\sum_{i=1}^{5}e_i=1-e_T.
\end{equation}
As in the earlier case, comparing this with \(\mathbb{P}(W^{\mathrm c}_f)=\mathbb{P}(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=0)=e_f\), we find that the target parities are opposite. Combining them gives:
\begin{equation}
1 = \mathbb{P}\Big(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=0\Big) + \mathbb{P}\Big(\bigoplus_{i=1}^{5}A^{(i)}_Z=1\Big) \geqslant 1-e_f + 1-e_T = 2-e,
\end{equation}
implying that \(e\geqslant 1\), which proves the necessary result following the same arguments as in the previous case.

All probabilities in the proof are conditional on (a fixed value of) the shared randomness \(\lambda\), and hence this is omitted. The players are indexed cyclically with indices modulo \(5\). Recall we notate the events corresponding to winning the (cyclic) three-player predicates and the all-\(Z\)-input predicate as 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Predicates_BP}
\begin{split}
W_i&\coloneqq A^{(i-1)}_X\oplus A^{(i)}_Z\oplus A^{(i+1)}_X=1,\,1\leqslant i\leqslant 5,\\
W_f&\coloneqq A^{(1)}_Z\oplus A^{(2)}_Z\oplus A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_Z = 0.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
For a given strategy \(\mathfrak S\), the probability of the event \(\{\mathfrak{S}\text{ wins on } Q_k\}\) is denoted as the probability \(\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(W_k)\) of the corresponding winning predicate given \(\mathfrak S\). Denote the probability of the event \(\{\mathfrak{S}\text{ loses on }Q_k\}\) as \(e_k\coloneqq \mathbb{P}_\mathfrak{S}(W^{\mathrm c}_k)\), for \(1\leqslant k\leqslant 5\) and \(e_f\coloneqq\mathbb{P}_\mathfrak{S}(W^{\mathrm c}_f)\). Also, let the sum of the loss probabilities on the five cyclic questions (where there are exactly three active players) be \(e_T = \sum_{k=1}^5 e_k\). The total probability of losing the game is then \(e=e_T + e_f\).

We first establish approximate determinism for the players choosing \(X\) as their input. Fix \(u\in\{1,\ldots,5\}\). The two three-player predicates involving \(A^{(u)}_X\) are \(A^{(u)}_X\oplus A^{(u+1)}_Z\oplus A^{(u+2)}_X=1\) and \(A^{(u+3)}_X\oplus A^{(u+4)}_Z\oplus A^{(u)}_X=1\), the respective failure probabilities of which are \(e_{u+1}\) and \(e_{u+4}\). In the global input context \((X,Z,X,X,Z)\) for the parties \((\mathsf{A}_u,\mathsf{A}_{u+1},\mathsf{A}_{u+2},\mathsf{A}_{u+3},\mathsf{A}_{u+4})\), the two predicates can hold simultaneously due to no-signaling, and so \(A^{(u+1)}_Z\oplus A^{(u+2)}_X\oplus A^{(u+3)}_X\oplus A^{(u+4)}_Z = 0\) holds, except with probability at most \(e_{u+1} + e_{u+4}\), which follows from the union bound for probabilities.

Moving to the inflated network, the relevant inflation marginal contains:
\begin{equation*}
A'^{(u)}_X, A'^{(u+1)}_Z, A'^{(u+2)}_X, A'^{(u+3)}_X, A'^{(u+4)}_Z, A''^{(u)}_X,
\end{equation*}
where \(A'^{(k)}_s,A''^{(k)}_s\) are the source-disjoint copies of \(A^{(k)}_s\), for \(k\in\{1,\ldots,5\}\) and \(s\in\{X,Z\}\). By the inflation isomorphism constraint, the inflation marginal satisfies:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathbb{P}(A'^{(u)}_X\oplus A'^{(u+1)}_Z\oplus A'^{(u+2)}_X\neq 1) &= e_{u+1},\\
\mathbb{P}(A'^{(u+3)}_X\oplus A'^{(u+4)}_Z\oplus A''^{(u)}_X\neq 1) &= e_{u+4},\\
\mathbb{P}(A'^{(u+1)}_Z\oplus A'^{(u+2)}_X\oplus A'^{(u+3)}_X\oplus A'^{(u+4)}_Z\neq 0) &\leqslant e_{u+1}+e_{u+4}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
If the three ``good events'', namely, 
\begin{equation*}
\{A'^{(u)}_X\oplus A'^{(u+1)}_Z\oplus A'^{(u+2)}_X = 1\}, \{A'^{(u+3)}_X\oplus A'^{(u+4)}_Z\oplus A''^{(u)}_X = 1\}, \{A'^{(u+1)}_Z\oplus A'^{(u+2)}_X\oplus A'^{(u+3)}_X\oplus A'^{(u+4)}_Z = 0\},
\end{equation*}
occur, then \(A'^{(u)}_X = A''^{(u)}_X\), and so by the union bound
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}(A'^{(u)}_X \neq A''^{(u)}_X)\leqslant 2(e_{u+1}+e_{u+4}).
\end{equation}
Since \(A'^{(u)}_X\) and \(A''^{(u)}_X\) are source-disjoint, they are independent, and since they are locally isomorphic copies of \(A^{(u)}_X\), they are identically distributed. By Lemma~\ref{l:PerfectParityAcrossCondIndSources_Robust}, there exists some \(\alpha_u\in\{0,1\}\) such that \(\mathbb{P}(A^{(u)}_X\neq\alpha_u)\leqslant 2(e_{u+1}+e_{u+4})\). Define \(d^X_u\coloneqq\mathbb{P}(A^{(u)}_X\neq\alpha_u)\) for the choice of $\alpha_u$ provided by the lemma for party $u$. Then \(d^X_u\leqslant 2(e_{u+1}+e_{u+4})\). Since this holds for any \(u\in\{1,\ldots,5\}\), summing over \(u\) gives:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:SumMismatchProb_Bnd}
\sum_{u=1}^{5}d^X_u \leqslant 2\sum_{u=1}^{5}(e_{u+1}+e_{u+4})=4e_T,
\end{equation}
where equality follows from the fact that \(i+1\mapsto i+4\) is a permutation of the indices, and so \(\sum_{u=1}^{5}(e_{u+1}+e_{u+4})=2e_T\).  

Consider now a strategy \(\mathfrak{S}'\) where any two players \(\mathsf{A}_u,\mathsf{A}_v\), for \(u\neq v\), behave deterministically on one of their inputs, say \(X\). One way to ``construct'' such a \(\mathfrak{S}'\) is to keep its internal protocol exactly the same as \(\mathfrak{S}\), but with the exception that players \(\mathsf{A}_u\) and \(\mathsf{A}_v\), instead of declaring their outputs as a function of the outcomes of all bipartite no-signaling resources and \(X\), now choose to ``overwrite'' it deterministically with \(A^{(u)}_X=\alpha_u\) and \(A^{(v)}_X=\alpha_v\), where \(\alpha_u,\alpha_v\in\{0,1\}\). Fix a question \(Q_i\). As shown in Table~\ref{tab:BPGame_Questions_ActiveSet}, the \textit{active set} \(I(Q_i)\) consists of the outputs of players involved in the winning predicate \(W_i\) corresponding to \(Q_i\). For any fixed \(Q_i\), following possibilities arise: (1) neither \(A^{(u)}_X\) nor \(A^{(v)}_X\) belongs to the active set, or (2) either \(A^{(u)}_X\) or \(A^{(v)}_X\) (or both) belongs to the active set. These possibilities can be formalized as the events \(\{A^{(u)}_X\notin I(Q_i)\}\cap\{A^{(v)}_X\notin I(Q_i)\}\), for the former, and \(\{A^{(u)}_X\in I(Q_i)\}\cup\{A^{(v)}_X\in I(Q_i)\}\), for the latter. When neither \(A^{(u)}_X\) nor \(A^{(v)}_X\) belongs to the active set, the probability of the event \(\{\mathfrak{S}\text{ wins on }Q_i\)\} is the same as that of \(\{\mathfrak{S}'\text{ wins on }Q_i\)\}, i.e., \(\mathbb{P}_\mathfrak{S}(W_i)=\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}'}(W_i)\), since the two strategies \(\mathfrak{S}\) and \(\mathfrak{S}'\) are identical in this case. Now suppose that \(A^{(u)}_X\in I(Q_i)\), but \(A^{(v)}_X\notin I(Q_i)\). If \(A^{(u)}_X=\alpha_u\) according to the original strategy \(\mathfrak{S}\), then the strategy \(\mathfrak{S}'\) does not contribute anything relevant to the winning predicate of \(Q_i\). (This is because, by construction, \(\mathfrak{S}'\) differs from \(\mathfrak{S}\) only in the replacement of the outcome \(A^{(u)}_X\) to \(\alpha_u\)). Then whenever \(\mathfrak{S}\) wins on \(Q_i\) but \(\mathfrak{S}'\) does not, it must be that \(A^{(u)}_X\neq \alpha_u\) in the original strategy. Equivalently, we have:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Au_X_belongsto_Qi}
\{\mathfrak{S}\text{ wins on }Q_i\}\subseteq\{\mathfrak{S}'\text{ wins on }Q_i\}\cup\{A^{(u)}_X\neq \alpha_u\text{ on }\mathfrak{S}\}\Rightarrow \mathbb{P}_\mathfrak{S}(W_i) \leqslant \mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}'}(W_i)+\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(A^{(u)}_X\neq\alpha_u).
\end{equation}
Next, suppose \(A^{(v)}_X\in I(Q_i)\) but \(A^{(u)}_X\notin I(Q_i)\). Using a similar reasoning as for the earlier case, we have:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Av_X_belongsto_Qi}
\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(W_i) \leqslant \mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}'}(W_i)+\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(A^{(v)}_X\neq\alpha_v).
\end{equation}
For the case \(A^{(u)}_X,A^{(v)}_X\in I(Q_i)\), we can adapt the above reasoning and obtain:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Au_X_Av_X_belongsto_Qi}
\begin{split}
\{\mathfrak{S}\text{ wins on }Q_i\}&\subseteq\{\mathfrak{S}'\text{ wins on }Q_i\}\cup\{A^{(u)}_X\neq\alpha_u\text{ on }\mathfrak{S}\}\cup\{A^{(v)}_X\neq\alpha_v\text{ on }\mathfrak{S}\}\\
\Rightarrow \mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(W_i)&\leqslant\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}'}(W_i)+\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(A^{(u)}_X\neq\alpha_u)+\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(A^{(v)}_X\neq\alpha_v).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
All the cases in~\eqref{eq:Au_X_belongsto_Qi},~\eqref{eq:Av_X_belongsto_Qi}, and~\eqref{eq:Au_X_Av_X_belongsto_Qi} together with the case \(A^{(u)}_X\notin I(Q_i),A^{(v)}_X\notin I(Q_i)\) can be expressed in a unified manner as shown below:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ProbIneq_S_S'}
\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(W_i)\leqslant\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}'}(W_i)+[\![A^{(u)}_X\in I(Q_i)]\!]\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(A^{(u)}_X\neq\alpha_u)+[\![A^{(v)}_X\in I(Q_i)]\!]\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(A^{(v)}_X\neq\alpha_v),
\end{equation}
which holds as an equality if \(A^{(u)}_X\notin I(Q_i)\) and \(A^{(v)}_X\notin I(Q_i)\). Following the definitions \(d^X_u\coloneqq\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(A^{(u)}_X\neq\alpha_u)\) and \(d^X_v\coloneqq\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(A^{(v)}_X\neq\alpha_v)\) for the mismatch probabilities, the inequality in~\eqref{eq:ProbIneq_S_S'} can be expressed equivalently as:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ProbIneq_S_S'_equiv}
\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(W_i)\leqslant\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}'}(W_i)+[\![A^{(u)}_X\in I(Q_i)]\!]d^X_u+[\![A^{(v)}_X\in I(Q_i)]\!]d^X_v.   
\end{equation}
Then summing over \(i\), and noting that \(\sum_{i=1}^6\mathbb{P}_{\mathfrak{S}}(W_i)=\mathcal{S}_{\mathfrak{S}}\), similarly for \(\mathfrak{S}'\), we get:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:BP_value_bound}
\mathcal{S}_{\mathfrak{S}}\leqslant \mathcal{S}_{\mathfrak{S}'}+\sum_{i=1}^{6}[\![A^{(u)}_X\in I(Q_i)]\!]d^X_u + \sum_{i=1}^{6}[\![A^{(v)}_X\in I(Q_i)]\!]d^X_v \leqslant \mathcal{S}_{\mathfrak{S}'}+2(d^X_u + d^X_v)\leqslant 5 + 2(d^X_u + d^X_v),
\end{equation}
where the second and third inequalities follow from Lemma~\ref{p:2DetPlayers} and the fact that for any given player \(\mathsf{A}_k\), the \(X\)-input outcome \(A^{(k)}_X\) appears in exactly two out of the five cyclic predicates. 

We now prove that \(2(d^X_u + d^X_v)\leqslant \frac{16}{5}e_T\), where \(e_T=\sum_{i=1}^{5}e_i\) is the sum of the failure probabilities of the five three-player predicates under strategy \(\mathfrak{S}\). This result combined with~\eqref{eq:BP_value_bound} will establish the required winning probability bound. There are \(\binom{5}{2}=10\) unordered pairs of players. Moreover, each mismatch probability \(d^X_u\) occurs in exactly four of the ten pair-sums \(d^X_u+d^X_v\), for \(u<v\). Therefore,
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{10}\sum_{1\leqslant u<v\leqslant 5}(d^X_u + d^X_v) = \frac{4}{10}\sum_{i=1}^{5}d^X_i\leqslant\frac{4}{10}(4e_T)=\frac{8}{5}e_T,
\end{equation}
where the inequality follows from the bound on the sum of mismatch probabilities in~\eqref{eq:SumMismatchProb_Bnd}. At least one pair-sum must be less than or equal to the average. Consequently, there exists a pair \(\{u,v\}\) such that
\begin{equation}
d^X_u + d^X_v \leqslant \frac{1}{10}\sum_{1\leqslant u<v\leqslant 5}(d^X_u + d^X_v) \leqslant \frac{8}{5}e_T, 
\end{equation}
which implies that \(2(d^X_u + d^X_v)\leqslant\frac{16}{5}e_T\). Notice that this need not hold for a pair \(\{u,v\}\) fixed beforehand. However, no pair was fixed. Since every pair is either adjacent or non-adjacent, and our two-players-deterministic-on-\(X\) obstruction covers both cases, the minimizing pair can be used. Therefore, combining this with~\eqref{eq:BP_value_bound} we get:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_{\mathfrak{S}}\leqslant 5 + \frac{16}{5}e_T\Rightarrow 6-(e_T + e_f)\leqslant 5 + \frac{16}{5}e_T \Rightarrow \frac{21}{5}e_T + e_f \geqslant 1.    
\end{equation}
Since \(0\leqslant e_f\leqslant 1\), it follows that \(\frac{21}{5}(e_T+e_f)\geqslant\frac{21}{5}e_T+e_f\geqslant1\), which implies \(e=e_T + e_f \geqslant \frac{5}{21}\). Since each question is sampled uniformly, this implies \(\frac{e}{6}\geqslant\frac{5}{126}\). Equivalently, \(\omega=\frac{1}{6}\mathcal{S}_{\mathfrak{S}}=\frac{1}{6}(6-e)\leqslant 1-\frac{5}{126}\).

Table~\ref{tab:Two4_wayNSboxes} defines two no-signaling boxes \(Q_0,Q_1\). Set
\begin{equation}
P_{r,s}(A^{(2)}_u,A^{(3)}_v,A^{(4)}_w,A^{(5)}_y) = Q_s(A^{(2)}_u\oplus ru,A^{(3)}_v,A^{(4)}_w,A^{(5)}_y\oplus ry).
\end{equation}
Thus the \(r=1\) branch flips \(\mathsf{A}_2\)'s and \(\mathsf{A}_5\)'s outputs precisely when their input is \(Z\). The no-signaling constraints are preserved by (local) relabelling of output. The affine support equations in Table~\ref{tab:Two4_wayNSboxes} verify~\eqref{eq:OneDetPlayerParityCond} for \(r=0\); the two \(Z\)-conditioned flips change the first line exactly as required for \(r=1\), preserve both three-player parities, and cancel in the four-player \(Z\)-parity. Substitution of \(\mathsf{A}_1\)'s output strategy then verifies all six predicates.

Suppose the five players share two uniform bits \((r,s)\). Relabel the inputs \(X,Z\) as \(0,1\), respectively. Player \(\mathsf{A}_1\) outputs deterministically as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:A_outcome_rule}
A^{(1)}_t = (1-t)r\oplus t(1\oplus s),\,\text{for input }t\in\{0,1\},
\end{equation}
and players \(\mathsf{A}_2,\mathsf{A}_3,\mathsf{A}_4,\mathsf{A}_5\) use \(P_{s}^{(r)}\), then the resulting behavior is exactly \(P_{\mathrm Q}\):
\begin{equation}
P_{\mathrm Q}(A^{(1)}_t,A^{(2)}_u,A^{(3)}_v,A^{(4)}_w,A^{(5)}_y) = \frac{1}{4}\sum_{r,s=0}^{1}[\![A^{(1)}_t = (1-t)r\oplus t(1\oplus s)]\!]P_{r,s}(A^{(2)}_u,A^{(3)}_v,A^{(4)}_w,A^{(5)}_y),
\end{equation}
where the Iverson bracket \([\![\Phi]\!]\) serves as an indicator function, evaluating to \(1\) when the condition \(\Phi\) holds and \(0\) otherwise.

Any no-signaling 2-party behavior \(P_{S^\lambda}\) in~\eqref{e:Svetlichny3} can be expressed as convex combination of behaviors which are either local deterministic or PR boxes~\cite{barrett:2005}. We can assume, without loss of generality, that each \(P_{S^\lambda}\) is one of these, by absorbing the convex weight over extremal no-signaling behaviors into \(\lambda\).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  
Now if \(P_{S_\lambda}(O_I)\) is local deterministic, \(P_{S_\lambda}(O_I)P_{S^{\mathrm c}_\lambda}(O^{\mathrm c}_{I^{\mathrm c}})\) is a distribution where two of the parties have fixed output, and so the winning probability is bounded by \(5/6\) by Proposition~\ref{p:2DetPlayers}. On the other hand, if \(P_{S_\lambda}(O_I)\) is a PR box, such a bipartite behavior has uniform output marginals, which, combined with the independence of the \(\lambda\)-indexed distribution across the \(S_\lambda|S^\mathrm{c}_\lambda\) split due to the factoring structure of the constituent 5-party distribution, makes it impossible to satisfy the predicate of~\eqref{e:5game} that contains parties in both \(S_\lambda\) and \(S^\mathrm{c}_\lambda\), at least with probability more than 5/6. The score is linear, so the result then extends to their convex mixtures and subsequently to the average over the shared randomness \(\lambda\).                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      

We explicitly prove the above claim about PR box case for the partitions \(S|S^{\mathrm c}=\{\mathsf{A}_1,\mathsf{A}_2\}|\{\mathsf{A}_3,\mathsf{A}_4,\mathsf{A}_5\}\) and \(\{\mathsf{A}_1,\mathsf{A}_3\}|\{\mathsf{A}_2,\mathsf{A}_4,\mathsf{A}_5\}\). This effectively covers all distinct cases via the cyclic symmetry of the predicates in \eqref{e:5game}. Let us start with the $I_\lambda=\{\mathsf{A}_1,\mathsf{A}_2\}$ case. The effective behavior of the adjacent players \(\mathsf{A}_1,\mathsf{A}_2\) is a PR box, which is defined, up to relabelling of inputs \(t,u\in\{0,1\}\) and outcomes \(a,b\in\{0,1\}\), as \(\mathrm{PR}(A^{(1)}_t=a,A^{(2)}_u=b)=[\![a\oplus b = t\cdot u]\!]/2\).) Since a PR box has uniform single-party marginals,
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}(A^{(1)}_t = 0) = \mathbb{P}(A^{(1)}_t = 1) = \frac{1}{2},\quad \mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_u = 0) = \mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_u = 1) = \frac{1}{2}.
\end{equation}
Of the five cyclic predicates, we find two where one player belongs to the \(\mathsf{A}_1\mathsf{A}_2\)-block and the remaining two belong to the \(\mathsf{A}_3\mathsf{A}_4\mathsf{A}_5\)-block. They are \(A^{(2)}_X\oplus A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_X = 1\) and \(A^{(4)}_X\oplus A^{(5)}_Z\oplus A^{(1)}_X=1\). Since \(A^{(2)}_X\) is uniform and independent of the \(\mathsf{A}_3\mathsf{A}_4\mathsf{A}_5\)-block (for the former), and \(A^{(1)}_X\) is uniform and independent of the \(\mathsf{A}_3\mathsf{A}_4\mathsf{A}_5\)-block, we have:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_X\oplus A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_X = 1) = \frac{1}{2},\quad
\mathbb{P}(A^{(4)}_X\oplus A^{(5)}_Z\oplus A^{(1)}_X = 1) = \frac{1}{2}.
\end{equation}
Each of the other four predicates has success probability at most \(1\). Therefore, the sum of the success probabilities $\mathcal S$ satisfies
\(\mathcal{S}\leqslant 4\times 1 + 2\times\frac{1}{2} = 5\), or equivalently, the win probability $\omega$ satisfies \(\omega\leqslant 5/6\).

Next, we prove the same result for the non-adjacent players \(\mathsf{A}_1,\mathsf{A}_3\). We observe that four of the five three-player predicates have one player belonging to the \(\mathsf{A}_1\mathsf{A}_3\)-block and the remaining two belonging to the \(\mathsf{A}_2\mathsf{A}_4\mathsf{A}_5\)-block. They are \(A^{(2)}_X\oplus A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_X=1,A^{(3)}_X\oplus A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_X=1, A^{(4)}_X\oplus A^{(5)}_Z\oplus A^{(1)}_X=1, A^{(5)}_X\oplus A^{(1)}_Z\oplus A^{(2)}_X=1\). Again, the \(2\)-party distribution is a PR box, whereby \(A^{(1)}_t\) is uniform and independent of the \(\mathsf{A}_2\mathsf{A}_4\mathsf{A}_5\)-block, for \(t\in\{X,Z\}\), and \(\mathsf{A}^{(3)}_v\) is uniform and independent of the \(\mathsf{A}_2\mathsf{A}_4\mathsf{A}_5\)-block for \(v\in\{X,Z\}\). We now have:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathbb{P}(A^{(2)}_X\oplus A^{(3)}_Z\oplus A^{(4)}_X=1)=\frac{1}{2},\quad \mathbb{P}(A^{(3)}_X\oplus A^{(4)}_Z\oplus A^{(5)}_X=1)=\frac{1}{2},\\
\mathbb{P}(A^{(4)}_X\oplus A^{(5)}_Z\oplus A^{(1)}_X=1)=\frac{1}{2},\quad \mathbb{P}(A^{(5)}_X\oplus A^{(1)}_Z\oplus A^{(2)}_X=1)=\frac{1}{2}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Each of the remaining two predicates have success probability at most \(1\). So \(\mathcal{S}\leqslant 4\times\frac{1}{2}+2\times 1=4\), and \(\omega\leqslant 2/3\).

\label{l:PerfectParityAcrossCondIndSources}
Let \(\lambda\) be a (shared) random variable distributed according to \(q(\lambda)\). For each \(\lambda\), let \(U,V\) be binary random variables whose conditional joint distribution factorizes as \(P_{U,V}(u,v|\lambda)=P^\lambda_U(u)P^\lambda_V(v)\). Suppose that for some fixed \(k\in\{0,1\}\), we have \(\mathbb{P}(U\oplus V=k)=1\). Then, for every \(\lambda\) with \(q(\lambda)>0\), there exists \(r_\lambda\in\{0,1\}\) such that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}_P(U=r_\lambda|\lambda) = 1,\quad \mathbb{P}_P(V=k\oplus r_\lambda|\lambda) = 1.
\end{equation}
Thus, \(U\text{ and }V\) are deterministic conditional on \(\lambda\), and their deterministic values have parity \(k\).

For each \(\lambda\), define the conditional failure probability \(e_\lambda\coloneqq \mathbb{P}_P(U\oplus V\neq k|\lambda)\). Since the unconditional parity relation holds perfectly, we have
\begin{equation*}
0 = \mathbb{P}(U\oplus V\neq k) = \sum_{\lambda\in\Lambda}q(\lambda)e_\lambda.
\end{equation*}
and since every summand is non-negative, we must have \(e_\lambda=0\) for every \(\lambda\) satisfying \(q(\lambda)>0\). Hence, it suffices to fix such a \(\lambda\) and prove the result for the product distribution \(P_{U,V}(u,v|\lambda)=P^\lambda_U(u)P^\lambda_V(v)\). Write \(p_\lambda\coloneqq \mathbb{P}_P(U=0|\lambda)\) and \(r'_\lambda\coloneqq \mathbb{P}_P(V=0|\lambda)\). Consider first \(k=0\). The condition \(U\oplus V=0\Rightarrow U=V\) with probability one. Hence,
\begin{align}
0 &=\mathbb{P}_P(U\neq V|\lambda)\nonumber \\ &=\mathbb{P}_P(U=0,V=1|\lambda)+\mathbb{P}_P(U=1,V=0|\lambda)\nonumber \\
&=p_\lambda(1-r'_\lambda)+(1-p\lambda)r'_{\lambda},
\end{align}
where the last equality follows from conditional independence. Since both terms are non-negative, they add up to zero only when both \(p_\lambda(1-r'_\lambda)=0\) and \((1-p\lambda)r'_{\lambda}=0\). The only two possible solutions are \(p_\lambda=r'_\lambda=0\) and \(p_\lambda=r'_\lambda=1\). Thus \(U\) and \(V\) are both deterministic and equal.

Now consider \(k=1\). The condition \(U\oplus V=1\Rightarrow U\neq V\) with probability one. Therefore,
\begin{align}
0 &=\mathbb{P}_P(U=V|\lambda)\nonumber \\ &=\mathbb{P}_P(U=0,V=0|\lambda)+\mathbb{P}_P(U=1,V=1|\lambda)\nonumber \\
&=p_\lambda r'_\lambda+(1-p\lambda)(1-r'_{\lambda}).    
\end{align}
Again, both terms are non-negative, so they add up to zero when both \(p_\lambda r'_\lambda=0\) and \((1-p\lambda)(1-r'_{\lambda})=0\). The only possible solutions are \((p_\lambda,r'_\lambda)=(1,0)\) and \((p_\lambda,r'_\lambda)=(0,1)\). Thus \(U\) and \(V\) are deterministic and opposite. In either case, there exists a bit \(r_\lambda\) such that \(U=r_\lambda\) and \(V=k\oplus r_\lambda\) with probability one conditional on \(\lambda\).

Let \(p\coloneqq \mathbb{P}(U=+1)\). Since \(U\text{ and }V\) are independent and identically distributed:
\begin{equation*}
\eta\geqslant \mathbb{P}(U\neq V) = p(1-p) + (1-p)p = 2p(1-p)\geqslant\delta,
\end{equation*}
where \(\delta\coloneqq\min\{p,1-p\}\). Choose \(u_\ast=0\) with \(p\geqslant1/2\), and \(u_\ast=-1\) otherwise. Then \(\mathbb{P}(U\neq u_\ast)=\delta\leqslant\eta\).
